============================================================
VISTA — Next Steps for EMNLP Paper Analysis
============================================================

CLAIM TO PROVE:
"Situational modifiers are also a part of decision making in
addition to scenario and the person's Schwartz value profile."

DATA AVAILABLE:
- 4 LLM analysis reports (Llama 3.3 70B, Qwen 2.5 32B, Gemma4 31B, Llama 3.1 8B)
- 1 dot-product rule-based baseline report
- 8,550 rows each (950 baseline + 7,600 modifier rows)
- 8 modifier axes, 10 scenarios, profile strength 0-9 HIGH values

KEY OBSERVATIONS FROM EXISTING DATA:
- LLM flip rates: 8.3% (Llama70B), 6.6% (Qwen), 8.9% (Gemma), 2.0% (Llama8B)
- Dot-product flip rate: 19.9% (over-predicts vs LLMs)
- All 3 large LLMs put Self-Preservation and Authority in top 2 axes
- Dot-product ranks Self-Preservation #6 — qualitative disagreement
- Dot-product mathematically forces 0% flips at 8-9 HIGH profiles
- LLMs flip 5-20% at 8-9 HIGH — "impossible flips" under value-only model

============================================================
STEP 1 — ESTABLISH A NOISE FLOOR (PARAPHRASE/SEED TEST)
============================================================

WHAT TO DO:
- Pick ~100 of the 950 baseline rows (no modifier attached).
- For each, write 3-5 paraphrased versions that mean the SAME thing.
  Example: "Would you accept the offer?" -> "Do you accept the offer?"
           -> "Should you take this offer?"
  No new information, just different wording.
- Re-run each LLM on all paraphrased versions.
- For each row, count how often the decision changes purely because
  the wording changed (or temperature/seed if sampled).
- Aggregate to get a "noise flip rate" per model.

WHY IT MATTERS:
LLMs are well-known to flip answers just because you reword a prompt.
If wording-only changes already flip 5% of decisions, our 6-9% modifier
rate is barely above noise and the paper falls apart. If the noise
floor is 1%, then 6-9% is clearly a real signal. This is the #1 reviewer
attack on this kind of work. Final claim becomes: "modifier flips
occur at X%, paraphrase noise at Y%, X significantly larger than Y."

PRIORITY: HIGHEST (defends against the #1 reviewer objection)
EFFORT: Medium (requires new LLM runs)

============================================================
STEP 2 — "IMPOSSIBLE FLIPS" HEADLINE TEST
============================================================

WHAT TO DO:
- For every modifier row, we already have two flip indicators:
  dot-product flip (yes/no) and LLM flip (yes/no).
- Create a new column:
  impossible_flip = (dot_product_flip == NO) AND (LLM_flip == YES)
- Stratify by profile strength (0 HIGH -> 9 HIGH).
  Build a table: rows = profile strength, columns = each LLM,
  cells = % of impossible flips.
- Zoom in on 8 HIGH and 9 HIGH rows specifically. Here dot-product
  score is mathematically guaranteed to predict no flip
  (0/320 and 0/160 cells in the dot-product report).
- Yet LLMs flip these cases:
  Llama70B: 10.6% (8H), 15.6% (9H)
  Gemma:    20.3% (8H), 15.6% (9H)
  Qwen:     10.3% (8H), 5.0%  (9H)
  Llama8B:  0.6%  (8H), 0.0%  (9H)

WHY IT MATTERS:
Dot-product is a stand-in for "decision fully determined by
value x scenario." When it predicts zero flips but LLM flips anyway,
those flips CANNOT be explained by value profile alone. That is
exactly our claim — modifiers contribute something BEYOND values
and scenarios — restated in unfalsifiable form. One simple table
or bar chart of this is the most powerful single piece of evidence
in the paper, because the reader doesn't need to trust our statistics;
the dot-product baseline is 0% by construction.

PRIORITY: HIGHEST (headline figure of the paper)
EFFORT: Low (pure reanalysis of existing data)

============================================================
STEP 3 — MCNEMAR PAIRED TEST (PER MODEL, PER AXIS)
============================================================

WHAT TO DO:
For each (model, axis) combination, build a 2x2 table:

                          | modifier -> A | modifier -> B
  baseline -> A           | A (no change) | B (flipped A->B)
  baseline -> B           | C (flipped B->A) | D (no change)

- Run McNemar's test: null hypothesis is B = C (flips happen
  equally in both directions, i.e., modifier has no systematic effect).
- Report p-value, odds ratio (B/C), and 95% CI on the odds ratio.
- Do this for all 9 axes x 4 models = 36 tests.
- Apply Bonferroni or Benjamini-Hochberg correction for multiple comparisons.

WHY IT MATTERS:
"8.3% of decisions flipped" is a description, not a test.
A reviewer asks: how do I know that's not chance? McNemar is the
textbook test for paired binary outcomes (same row, two conditions:
baseline vs modifier), which is exactly our design. The odds ratio
with CI gives an effect-size number we can compare meaningfully
across axes and models: "Authority signals shift decisions 3.2x more
than baseline, 95% CI [2.1, 4.8]". This is the language reviewers
expect; without it the paper looks like exploratory data analysis,
not hypothesis testing.

PRIORITY: HIGH (standard statistical legitimacy)
EFFORT: Low (pure reanalysis)

============================================================
STEP 4 — MIXED-EFFECTS LOGISTIC REGRESSION (VARIANCE DECOMPOSITION)
============================================================

WHAT TO DO:
- Pool all 8,550 rows into one dataset (per model).
- Fit a logistic regression. Outcome = binary decision made by LLM.
  Predictors:
    * scenario (categorical, 10 levels)
    * value_profile (10 Schwartz value scores, or count of HIGH values)
    * modifier_axis (categorical, 8 levels + "none")
    * Random intercept per profile_id (controls for repeated profiles)
- Fit a REDUCED model that omits the modifier_axis term.
- Compare full vs reduced models with a likelihood-ratio test (LR test).
- Report p-value, delta AIC, delta BIC.

WHY IT MATTERS:
Our claim has three pieces: scenario, value profile, AND modifier.
The LR test answers the precise question: "After scenario and value
profile already explain what they can, does adding a modifier term
significantly improve the model's fit?" If yes (low p-value,
large delta AIC), then modifiers explain variance that scenario
and values cannot — which is the claim in formal statistical form.
This is the single result that maps most directly onto the claim
wording and is the strongest single sentence in the results section.

PRIORITY: HIGHEST (the formal version of the exact claim)
EFFORT: Low-Medium

============================================================
STEP 5 — AXIS-RANKING DIVERGENCE (LLM vs DOT-PRODUCT)
============================================================

WHAT TO DO:
- For each model, rank the 8 modifier axes from highest flip rate (1)
  to lowest (8).
- Do the same for the dot-product baseline.
- Compute Spearman rank correlation between LLM ranking and
  dot-product ranking, per model.
- Build a side-by-side table showing each axis's rank in each model.
- Highlight axes where rank differs by >= 3 positions
  (qualitative disagreements).

EXPECTED FINDING:
Dot-product ranks: Authority(1), In/Out-Group(2), Social Vis(3),
  Competence(4), Scarcity(5), Self-Pres(6), Time(7), Diffused(8)
Llama70B ranks: Self-Pres(1), Authority(2), Scarcity(3), ...
Self-Preservation jumps from #6 (dot-product) to #1/#2 (all 3 large LLMs).

WHY IT MATTERS:
A reviewer might say: "Maybe the LLM is just a noisy dot-product."
If true, LLM axis ranking would match dot-product ranking. But LLMs
put Self-Preservation at #1/#2 while dot-product puts it at #6.
That's not a noisy version of the same thing — it's a DIFFERENT
decision rule. This rules out the simplest competing explanation
for our data and lets us claim the LLM is integrating modifiers
in a non-trivial way.

PRIORITY: HIGH (kills the "LLM = noisy dot-product" objection)
EFFORT: Low

============================================================
STEP 6 — CROSS-MODEL CONSISTENCY
============================================================

WHAT TO DO:
- Take the 8-axis ranking for each of the 4 LLMs.
- Compute pairwise Spearman rho between every pair of models
  (6 pairs total). Build a 4x4 correlation matrix.
- Report average pairwise rho.
- Flag axes that consistently appear in top 2 or bottom 2
  across all models.

EXPECTED FINDING:
Self-Preservation and Authority are top 2 across Llama (Meta),
Qwen (Alibaba), and Gemma (Google) — three different labs,
three different training pipelines.

WHY IT MATTERS:
If only Llama produced the Self-Preservation effect, a reviewer
could say "this is an artifact of Meta's specific RLHF process."
But Llama (Meta), Qwen (Alibaba), and Gemma (Google) are trained
by three different labs with three different alignment pipelines
and three different data mixes. If they AGREE on which axes are
most influential, the effect is a property of the STIMULUS and
the underlying social phenomenon, not of any one company's training
choices. This is built-in independent replication — one of the
strongest defenses against the "single-model quirk" objection.

PRIORITY: HIGH (built-in independent replication)
EFFORT: Low

============================================================
STEP 7 — SCALE EFFECT (DON'T HIDE THE 8B RESULT)
============================================================

WHAT TO DO:
- Make a simple line plot:
  x-axis = log(parameter count), y-axis = overall flip rate.
  Four points: 8B (2.0%), 32B (6.6%), 31B (8.9%), 70B (8.3%).
- Repeat per top axis (Self-Preservation, Authority).
- In the discussion, explicitly address two competing interpretations.

TWO STORIES TO DISCUSS:
1. CAPABILITY STORY: small models don't fully "understand" the modifiers;
   sensitivity emerges with capability.
2. ALIGNMENT STORY: smaller models are more heavily RLHF'd toward
   "consistent, principled" answers, which suppresses modifier
   sensitivity. Large models retain more of the social-psychology pull.

WHY IT MATTERS:
Both interpretations are interesting and publishable. We don't have
to resolve which is right — speculating is fine. But if we OMIT the
8B result, a reviewer will run their own experiment and call us on
it. Including it and naming the two interpretations is more credible
and gives us a second-order finding worth publishing.

PRIORITY: MEDIUM (defensive — pre-empts reviewer running own check)
EFFORT: Trivial

============================================================
STEP 8 — QUALITATIVE AUDIT (THE PART HUMANS READ)
============================================================

WHAT TO DO:
- Sample ~50 rows where:
  (a) profile is strong (4+ HIGH)
  (b) the modifier is one of the top two axes (Self-Pres or Authority)
  (c) the LLM flipped its decision
- If reasoning isn't already captured, re-run those 50 rows with a
  "explain your reasoning, then answer" prompt.
- Hand-code each example with a simple checklist:
  * Did the reasoning CITE the modifier?
  * Was the reasoning coherent?
  * Did the model explicitly TRADE OFF the value against the situation?
- Pick 3-5 representative examples for the paper, including:
  the prompt, the baseline answer, the modifier-condition answer,
  and the model's reasoning.

WHY IT MATTERS:
Statistical results alone can always be dismissed as "the model is
doing something random and the modifier just happens to correlate
with the flip." If we can show that in 40 out of 50 cases the model
explicitly mentions the authority figure or the danger when justifying
the flip, we've shown the modifier is CAUSALLY DRIVING the decision
in the model's own reasoning, not just correlated with it. Quoted
examples also do something statistics cannot: they make the phenomenon
vivid and undeniable to a reader skimming the paper.

PRIORITY: MEDIUM (makes the phenomenon real to the reader)
EFFORT: Medium (hand coding)

